ount over 256 buckets and then find most frequent with tie-breaking? But need exact semantics: The original code sorts labels (char values) ascending, then counts consecutive equal labels; if two labels have same max frequency, the one that appears first in sorted order wins because it updates only when current_frequency > most_frequent_count (strictly greater). So ties go to smaller label value. We can replicate by counting frequencies and scanning from 0 to 255, updating on strictly greater. That would be O(k + 256) which is faster than qsort for small k? Actually qsort of k chars is very fast; but maybe still overhead. Could try.\n\nBut role B is about SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies, not sorting. However we can include it as a transformation if it helps performance without violating role? The instruction says \"Propose one candidate relevant to your role.\" So should focus on SIMD/loop deps. But we can still make other optimizations as long as they are legal.\n\nMaybe combine: Use counting sort for labels and also add restrict pointers. That might give a small improvement.\n\nLet's propose:\n- Add `restrict` qualifiers to local pointer variables in knn() after allocation.\n- Replace qsort-based frequency count with direct bucket counting using an array of 256 ints, scanning labels once. This avoids sorting overhead and is deterministic (ties broken by smaller label due to ascending scan).\n- Also align heap arrays? Not necessary.\n\nBut we must ensure exact output matches original for all inputs. The original sorts labels then counts; if two labels have same max frequency, the one with smaller char value wins because sorted order puts it first and update only on strictly greater count. Our bucket counting scanning from 0 to 255 will also pick smallest label among ties because we update only when current_count > best_count (strictly). So correct.\n\nLet's verify: Original code:\n```\nqsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\nchar most_frequent =